PREFACE

  Directive 8 of 2011

  Directive amending Directive 12 of 2004 on the protection of personal data

  Directive of the Assembly of the Hesperian Union and of the Hesperian Council amending Directive 12 of 2004 as regards the notification of personal data breaches, the powers of supervisory authorities and the deletion of the provision on data protection by design.

PREAMBLE

  Having regard to the Treaty establishing the Hesperian Union, and in particular Article 41 thereof,

  Having regard to the proposal from the Hesperian Commission,

  Whereas:

  (1) Directive 12 of 2004 laid down the rules relating to the protection of natural persons with regard to the processing of personal data; experience of its application has shown that certain of its provisions should be strengthened and others clarified.

  (2) The period within which a controller is required to notify a personal data breach to the competent supervisory authority should be shortened, in order to enable the authority and the data subjects concerned to respond to the breach in good time.

  (3) The powers of supervisory authorities should be reinforced by an express power to impose administrative fines, so that penalties for infringement are effective, proportionate and dissuasive throughout the Union.

  (4) The obligation of data protection by design is now comprehensively addressed by a separate instrument of the Union, and the corresponding provision of Directive 12 of 2004 has become redundant and should be deleted.

  (5) It is appropriate to insert a provision on the designation of a data protection officer, in order to assist controllers and processors in monitoring compliance with the rules on the protection of personal data.

  THE ASSEMBLY OF THE HESPERIAN UNION AND THE HESPERIAN COUNCIL, Having regard to the Treaty establishing the Hesperian Union, HAS ADOPTED THIS DIRECTIVE:

BODY

ARTICLE 1 - Amendment of Article 15

  Article 15 of Directive 12 of 2004 is replaced by the following: "In the case of a personal data breach, the controller shall without undue delay, and where feasible not later than forty-eight hours after having become aware of it, notify the breach to the competent supervisory authority; where the breach is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons, the controller shall also communicate the breach to the data subject without undue delay and in plain language.".

ARTICLE 2 - Amendment of Article 18

  In Article 18(2) of Directive 12 of 2004, after the words "including a ban, on processing" there shall be inserted the words ", and to impose administrative fines which shall be effective, proportionate and dissuasive".

ARTICLE 3 - Deletion of Article 16

  Article 16 of Directive 12 of 2004 is deleted.

ARTICLE 4 - Insertion of Article 16a

  After Article 16 of Directive 12 of 2004 there shall be inserted the following Article: "ARTICLE 16a. The controller and the processor shall designate a data protection officer where the core activities of the controller or the processor consist of processing operations which require regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a large scale; the data protection officer shall be designated on the basis of professional qualities and expert knowledge of the law and practice relating to the protection of personal data, and shall report directly to the highest management level of the controller or the processor.".

ARTICLE 5 - Transposition

  PARAGRAPH (1)

    Member states shall bring into force the laws, regulations and administrative provisions necessary to comply with this Directive by 30 June 2013, and shall immediately inform the Commission thereof.

  PARAGRAPH (2)

    When member states adopt those measures, they shall contain a reference to this Directive or be accompanied by such a reference on the occasion of their official publication.

ARTICLE 6 - Entry into force

  This Directive shall enter into force on the twentieth day following that of its publication in the Official Journal of the Hesperian Union, and is addressed to the member states.
